and they used those  valuation techniques with a lot of  heroic benefits transfer I guess you've  heard about benefits transfer  to try and assign values to those  pollution pollutants and use of  resources and this is what they came up  with and I guess Pavan may have gone  into this a little bit but essentially  because the data that's available is  very heavily weighted towards greenhouse  gases the green portions of the bars are  basically the bulk of the problem in  their analysis and those are greenhouse  gas emissions as the value of greenhouse  gas emissions using I believe the social  accounting price for carbon not the  market price I assume you've discussed  the difference between the social price  of carbon and the market price of carbon  yes thank you food producers as you  would expect come out heavily weighted  by water use no surprise there what  strikes me however is that again  biodiversity Nemo's other ecosystem  services related to biodiversity don't  figure here and that's just a it's a  reflection of the data that is available  if you look at US EPA's input-output  data they don't tell you about use of  ecosystem services or impacts on  biodiversity so and even if they did we  don't have prices for most of those  ecosystem services so it would be very  difficult to construct a table like this  that's an area that still needs work  here's an example not at a global level  but at a country level and a sector this  is forestry in China Pavan you didn't  use this one I don't think no ok so here  they the analysts again true cost and  this was done on behalf of teeb tried to  look at a broader range of ecosystem  services related to deforestation and to  link that to logging and the use of  timber in the Chinese economy during the  60s 70s and 80s and 90s and they came up  with an estimate of the loss of  ecosystem services resulting from  deforestation that could be attributed  to the production  Timber for the materials in construction  industry in China and then they said  okay let's add up those environmental  costs and compare it to the market price  and you can see in the chart on the left  that the environmental costs are greater  than the the market price of timber in  Beijing in 1998 which is interesting  partly because the market price in 1998  did not reflect any of those ecosystem  impacts so that market price the the  green bar and that was the costs of you  know accessing the land extracting the  timber producing it shipping it to  Beijing in some semi-finished form in  cubic meters dollars per cubic meter it  did not include those environmental  impacts and if those environmental  impacts had been reflected in the market  price of timber you would imagine a lot  of changes in the the use of timber in  China now what's interesting also is  that around about the same time there  were some environmental disasters in  China including major flooding which was  attributed to deforestation and thus to  logging and there was a logging ban  imposed shortly after 1998 I think in  1998 as a result of which - you could  say that the logging ban took account of  these environmental impacts and  corrected the market failure but you  could also point to the fact that the  consumption of timber in China did not  go down the construction boom continued  is just they found their timber  somewhere else they imported it so those  environmental costs have been shifted  from Chinese forests to other forests  and you'd have to look at well where is  the construction industry in China now  getting its timber from and are those  countries managing their forests in an  appropriate way do they have policies  that reflect these environmental impacts  unfortunately in many cases they don't  so I talked a lot about business impacts  on the environment and mostly I realized  I've talked about negative impacts I'll  get back to positive later on but I  think it's another area that people tend  not to talk about enough in my view is  the dependence of business on  biodiversity and ecosystem services and  this is one very obvious example in  agriculture is the role of wild insects  and other animals in pollination and the  value that that delivers to agriculture  now we know that the value of food  includes the value of pollination  because without the pollination there's  no food but typically in many parts of  the world farmers do not pay for  pollination services interestingly in  the u.s. for some crops they do for  almonds in California for blueberries in  this case in Michigan farmers will pay  for pollination services and we can see  from that what's the value to in terms  of the value-added of those pollinators  to agriculture in the u.s. it's very  often domesticated bees that are rented  for the purpose people will rent the  hive the bees will come and pollinate  the crop the farmer then pays the owner  of the beehives who moves on to the next  farm the problem is you probably know is  that in North America and some other  continents we have a problem with  domesticated bees namely they're dying  right so this colony collapse disorder  then has generated more interest in how  do we maintain populations of wild bees  and wild pollination services and the  case study that we we have in teeb is  from Syngenta which is an agribusiness  company they produce chemicals and seeds  for farming and they realize that the  health of their business depends on the  health of farmers and the health of  farmers depends on the health of  pollinators and so they've invested with  the US Department of Agriculture and  others in something called I think  operation Paul  which is an attempt to try and restore  habitat for wild pollinators as a kind  of backstop if we don't find a solution  to the whatever's affecting domesticated  bees will at least have the wild  pollinators to maintain production to  some extent okay  I've talked a lot probably too much and  I should have said at the outset that  I'm very happy for anyone to interrupt  me at any time  and say we know all that or to ask a  question or to disagree or whatever you  wish to do yes I would agree with that  my experience of working with some some  big companies is that they're slightly  schizophrenic just like any big  institution like the US government  you'll find people that hold very  different points of view within the same  organization so the people working on  health safety and environment or  sustainability they will have a very  different set of priorities but they  don't generally run the show they don't  have as much influence say as the  financial directors and other chief  other executives I think the the Global  Reporting Initiative is on the right  track this is a very simplistic approach  what they've got here but this is an  attempt is it I don't know if this is  the answer to your question but they're  trying to boil it down to a few products  or resources that would be intelligible  to most companies the ones listed on the  left there and then a few simple ways to  measure those now I'm not convinced that  all of these are going to work so for  example look at fibre which would  include most forestry pulp and paper and  they want they're interested in the  volume or weight or the area planted  well that's not gonna get us very close  to biodiversity is it I mean so I think  there's still a lot of work to be done  and I'll talk a little bit next week  about what's called a habitat hectares  approach which is kind of area with a  quality adjustment  you sound like you're from Australia  okay well that's where it comes from as  you probably know the habitat Hector's  approach and they're people who've  developed similar metrics here in the  u.s. related to wetland mitigation  banking and conscious species  conservation banking schemes it's a  similar approach but there's still a  long way to go there's a lot in that  question because I think what consumers  need is different from what the business  needs business can probably afford to  dive deeper get more technical more  detailed consumer just want something  they can quickly eyeball in the  supermarket or without having to think  too much about what's inside that  they're more concerned perhaps about  credibility is it independently verified  as opposed to self certified I think  it's instructive to look at the example  of climate change and what happened  there it's simpler because you can take  16 greenhouse gases or however many they  took and boil them down to co2  equivalent it's fairly straightforward  the chemistry is not complicated  biodiversity is never gonna be that  simple so you're never gonna be able to  boil it down to one indicator I don't  think you probably need to look at  sector specific sets of indicators but  what is instructive about the the  greenhouse gas example is at one stage  probably in the mid 90s the World  Resources Institute based in Washington  an NGO got together with the World  Business Council for sustainable  development I mentioned them before a  big business umbrella group and came up  with a protocol for measuring greenhouse  gases in business and I think you need  something like that that has the  scientific credibility from the  biodiversity crowd but also the business  credibility of an organization like WBC  SD to say this is the protocol this is  how you measure it and this is best  practice it doesn't have to be  government that does that but there has  to be buy-in from both the scientific  side and the business side yeah I think  that's that well as I said if you don't  do that you end up with these kind of  indicators you know I've been working in  IUCN for nine years with a lot of  ecologists and constant  and biologists and and land use  management specialists and they come up  with great indicators but they're at a  different level they're good for sort of  landscape level national level  assessments and and planning but they're  not adapted to the needs of a big  business or a little business okay I  will move on in that case I have a  little case study about valuing  ecosystem impacts positive and actually  in this case part mostly positive some  negative and this was a case study  carried out as a contribution to that  wbcs d guide I mentioned on how to use  valuation in in a corporate context they  had what we call I'll go back to that  briefly  they had Road testers so those logos on  the bottom are the companies that  volunteered to try out these valuation  techniques that you learned about the  last few weeks and apply them to their  situation so you've got many different  sectors you've got agribusiness  environmental services for a pulp and  paper mining pulp and paper again cement  to cement companies electronics Escom is  a big southern African power producer  they have mainly Coal Fired power plants  some hydro Eni is an oil and gas  producer EDP I forget you've got  chemicals ASCO Nobel pharmaceuticals and  so on so you've got a range of different  sectors they all agreed to say alright  we'll partner up with some valuation  specialists people who know how to value  ecosystem services and see if we can  come up with something interesting and  IUCN was part of that and one of the  case studies that we did as part of this  project was with wholesome big Cement  Company they have in the United Kingdom  in Yorkshire some quarries from which  they extract sand and gravel big  component in concrete as you know and  they were interested in  how to maximize the environmental  benefit of the restoration and Rehab  that they are required by law to do as a  condition for getting a permit to dig  sand and gravel and so they weren't  wondered whether valuation could help  them in that planning process of  figuring out what is the best  restoration and rehabilitation option  for them and they would claim at Holcim  that they look at biodiversity  throughout the project cycle and I think  it's it's 